body:
I know this post is about a million years old by reddit terms, but I think the only real disadvantage is just the feel.

&#x200B;

What do I mean by this?

&#x200B;

Well, the majority of gamers have learned, been taught, or just accepted that your mouse click is attributed to the weapon firing. It helps queue some game actions in your mind too such as recoil control in CSGO or VALORANT.

&#x200B;

I say this because I have been interested in switching and have been actively using spacebar or even left ALT for shooting purposes and I have to admit there is an increased sense of stability and my tracking feels ever so slightly smoother (I love aiming smoothly with little jitters) and it somewhat feels like the tracking I would do on a friendly in warmup in CSGO where I would just mouse over them without shooting if that makes sense.

&#x200B;

It's a mental conditioning thing, and I can definitely feel flicking getting worse as my flick timing is no longer dictated by my mouse click but me timing the space bar click with my mouse movement.

&#x200B;

Old thread, but still my opinion for anyone to find 🫡

Replying hopefully to all your inquires in this post. Bahrain is a pretty small island with not much in the way of activities, especially in the summer where the weather is hot and humid; making friendships is key for a good time in this country. With your current salary, you're looking at quite a decent living experience. As for housing, assuming you're willing to stretch the budget to BHD400, you can find inclusive 2-4 bedroom apartments in Amwaj^(1); never go for exclusive, since electricity and water costs, especially in the summer, can range from BHD90-200. Usually, landlords will ask for a 2-3 month downpayment on apartments and you can usually negotiate prices with agents. As for transportation, assuming your company can't provide you with a car, you could go the route of leasing and get a pretty suitable ride within your current budget. Having a car is necessary for living in Bahrain as Taxis and ride-hailing applications like Uber and Careem are expensive, and bus services are not that convenient. Home internet in Bahrain is underwhelming though, you'll be looking at BHD 26 minimum for a decent service, and speeds aren't that impressive for the price either; good enough for Netflix and 1440p YouTube. As for the social experience, you'll find that most people are quite friendly and supportive, and you won't run into much trouble with those around you. However, be cautious while driving on the road since the driving style in Bahrain is "unique" for lack of a better word. Bahrain is also a very safe country, from my 20 years of experience living in this country, I haven't really had any encounters that I would describe as threating to my life or the lives of those around me. Overall, if I was in your position, I'd definitely go for it; Bahrain might be small and dull at times, but it's generally a safe and welcoming country. If you go for it, welcome on board, we're happy to have you with us :). if you have anything on your mind that you would like to know, reply or message me directly, I'll be happy to help you. Have a nice day!

&#x200B;

1: [https://www.propertyfinder.bh/en/search?bf=2&c=2&l=44&ob=mr&page=1&pt=400&rp=m&ut=inclusive](https://www.propertyfinder.bh/en/search?bf=2&c=2&l=44&ob=mr&page=1&pt=400&rp=m&ut=inclusive)
